 1. **The operator's gates come first.** The grant covers only the queue's items, so items the operator names as theirs and PRs outside the queue stop at merge-ready. A request to state the plan is not a go.
 2. **Spawn one owner per PR, and keep its work where a replacement can find it.** Resolve the forge once for the program. GitHub CLI (`gh`) is the default. If `command -v origin` succeeds and Origin can resolve the repository, use `origin pr` for every PR operation. Otherwise stay on `gh` and record the fallback. Never require Graphite (`gt`). Each owner is a subagent spawned with `isolation: "worktree"` that builds, opens its PR per `playbooks/opening-a-pr.md`, proves the change on the real artifact, babysits to green per `playbooks/babysit.md`, and merges once steps 4 and 5 clear it.
 
-   Open the PR early so CI and bots run alongside the work. The code is ready only after the slop-strip (Claude Code's built-in `/simplify` over the diff, then remove what it leaves of narrating comments, unsupported guards, dead compatibility paths, and unrelated edits) and the `/no-comments` pass, so the full swarm runs on finished code. Then rebase onto current trunk and report the code-ready head before self-proof, so verification runs on the base the patch lands on. Report every later head, and merge-ready once self-proof, CI, and babysit are done. Fix rounds stay on that base unless trunk conflicts, breaks CI, or lands a known concurrent change to the same files, because each rebase restarts CI. Run the repo's checks for the touched paths on the committed head before the code-ready push and before a later push that needs a verifier.
+   Open the PR early so CI and bots run alongside the work. The code is ready only after the slop-strip (Claude Code's built-in `/simplify` over the diff, then remove what it leaves of narrating comments, unsupported guards, dead compatibility paths, and unrelated edits), the `/no-comments` pass, and Claude Code's built-in `/code-review high` over the branch, so the full swarm runs on finished code. Then rebase onto current trunk and report the code-ready head before self-proof, so verification runs on the base the patch lands on. Report every later head, and merge-ready once self-proof, CI, and babysit are done. Fix rounds stay on that base unless trunk conflicts, breaks CI, or lands a known concurrent change to the same files, because each rebase restarts CI. Run the repo's checks for the touched paths on the committed head before the code-ready push and before a later push that needs a verifier.
 
    Assume the VM dies. Soon after starting, push your branch and start a decision trail in `<root>/.nstack/autopilot/<slug>/`, one file per owner, per the **show-me-your-work** skill, then push each finished unit before you start the next. Whoever spawns a subagent records its id, state, and a stuck-after runtime from past runs of its kind in its file there, so the root can probe it if the parent dies. Force-push only your own branch, with `--force-with-lease` pinned to the SHA you last pushed or took over, and on any push you did not make, stop and report to the root.
 3. **Run owners in parallel off trunk, never stacked,** so each PR lands on its own.
